WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Let's spend the next few minutes going over how Claude actually picks a tool from the available

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:11.039
palette, and what happens when that pick goes wrong. We'll look at the two main failure modes,

3
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:16.479
the description writing technique that prevents them, and how to use production logging to catch

4
00:00:16.479 --> 00:00:23.200
routing degradation before it becomes a problem. When routing goes wrong, it fails in recognizable

5
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:29.200
patterns, and naming them is what makes them fixable. A no good match failure happens when

6
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.959
none of the available tool descriptions closely match the user's request. The model might fabricate

7
00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:41.040
a tool name, refuse to act, or call the least wrong option, and silently produce bad output.

8
00:00:41.759 --> 00:00:47.680
Those silent errors are the hardest ones to catch. A two good matches failure happens when two tools

9
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.360
both look like valid candidates. The model alternates between them across turns on requests

10
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.840
that look identical, which makes behavior non-deterministic and hard to diagnose.

11
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.599
Disambiguation patterns are the description writing techniques that make each tool the

12
00:01:03.599 --> 00:01:10.000
unambiguous winner for its intended request type. That vocabulary is your diagnostic kit for any

13
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.720
routing problem you hit. It helps to think of tool selection as a matching problem. On every turn,

14
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.480
the model reads the incoming request and scores each available tool's description for relevance.

15
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:29.839
The highest scoring match wins, and the model calls that tool. Notice what's not in this process.

16
00:01:29.839 --> 00:01:35.680
The model doesn't run the tool and check whether the output looks right. It commits to a selection

17
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:41.199
based purely on descriptions. That's why description quality is the primary lever

18
00:01:41.199 --> 00:01:47.839
for improving selection accuracy. A tool that can do exactly what the user needs will still

19
00:01:47.839 --> 00:01:54.720
lose out at selection time if its description is vague, generic, or uses vocabulary that doesn't

20
00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:00.559
align with how users phrase their requests. Writing descriptions is a form of precision

21
00:02:00.559 --> 00:02:07.440
engineering, not documentation. The most reliable fix for both failure modes is writing descriptions

22
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:14.160
that make each tool the unambiguous winner for its intended request type. The two core techniques

23
00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.399
are distinct verbs and narrow scopes. Distinct verbs means each tool description starts with a

24
00:02:20.399 --> 00:02:27.199
verb that doesn't apply to any other tool in the palette. Retrieves, writes, deletes, summarizes,

25
00:02:27.199 --> 00:02:34.399
translates. These verbs signal intent clearly and rarely overlap. Narrow scope means you tell the

26
00:02:34.399 --> 00:02:40.639
model not just what the tool does, but what it doesn't do. Retrieves records from the user

27
00:02:40.639 --> 00:02:47.360
database, doesn't write or update records. That negative boundary is often more useful than the

28
00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.800
positive description because it's exactly what the model needs to break a tie between two similar

29
00:02:52.800 --> 00:03:00.080
tools. Tool descriptions carry most of the routing weight, but the system prompt can reinforce them

30
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:06.080
when descriptions alone aren't enough. One effective pattern is adding an explicit routing

31
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:12.399
section to the system prompt. This section names each tool and states when to use it versus the

32
00:03:12.399 --> 00:03:18.479
alternatives. It's particularly useful when you have tools that are similar by nature and can't

33
00:03:18.479 --> 00:03:24.960
be distinguished by description alone. Tie-breaking instructions are another technique. When two tools

34
00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:31.360
are genuinely close in scope, you can instruct the model to prefer one based on a specific criterion.

35
00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.720
For example, prefer the cached retrieval tool when the data is less than five minutes old.

36
00:03:37.279 --> 00:03:43.279
Negative examples round out the toolkit. Listing which tool not to call for common edge cases

37
00:03:43.279 --> 00:03:48.559
gives the model a concrete guide for the ambiguous situations your descriptions don't

38
00:03:48.559 --> 00:03:55.199
fully resolve. Routing quality doesn't stay constant. As you add tools, rename them or

39
00:03:55.199 --> 00:04:00.639
adjust descriptions. Selection behavior changes, and users change how they phrase requests over

40
00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:07.759
time too. That gradual decay is selection drift, and it's invisible until something breaks. Logging

41
00:04:07.759 --> 00:04:14.320
is how you catch it early. The basic implementation is straightforward. Log every tool call with the

42
00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.920
tool name and the request that triggered it. Over time, you can track selection frequency for each

43
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.839
tool. A tool that drops from 10 calls an hour to two without a matching change in user behavior

44
00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:31.920
is a signal that routing has shifted. Maybe a newer tool is stealing its requests. Maybe a

45
00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.519
description change introduced ambiguity. Either way, the log gives you the data to investigate

46
00:04:37.519 --> 00:04:43.040
instead of hearing about it from user complaints. One of the most common mistakes teams make when a

47
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.640
tool isn't being selected correctly is adding a new tool to cover the gap. That's almost always

48
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:54.880
the wrong move. Adding a tool grows the catalog, which increases context overhead and introduces

49
00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.880
new opportunities for overlap. Before you add, ask whether the existing tool could cover the need

50
00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.480
if its description were refined. In most cases, the answer is yes. A better description fixes the

51
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.839
selection problem without any of the downsides. The rule I'd suggest is to default to refinement

52
00:05:11.839 --> 00:05:17.200
and add a tool only when the use case is genuinely outside the scope of anything you already have.

53
00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.720
When you do add, write the new tool's description and the neighboring tool's descriptions at the

54
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:29.519
same time to make sure the boundaries are clean from the start. Selection comes down to scoring

55
00:05:29.519 --> 00:05:36.000
text. The model reads the request, ranks each description for fit, and calls the winner. Routing

56
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.359
accuracy is therefore something you engineer in the wording, mostly with distinct verbs and an

57
00:05:41.359 --> 00:05:47.440
honest boundary on what a tool won't touch. One thing to watch, the accuracy decays quietly as

58
00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:54.079
the catalog grows. So log every selection and read pattern. That brings this one to a close.

59
00:05:54.079 --> 00:05:55.359
I'll see you in the next one.

